md prints the usage of the whole tool, not of the subcommand that
refused:

    Usage: md <subcommand>
      See 'md --help' for more information.

So a user who mistypes an index is told to read the help of `md`, and
is never shown the arguments of the command they actually ran.

The cause is where the guard sits. RemoveCommand checks the
index inside run(), and a ValidationError thrown there reaches
MessageInfo as a bare error rather than as a CommandError, so the
command stack has been lost and falls back to the root command. The
same command's --in-place with --stdin guard is thrown from validate(),
keeps its command stack, and does name the subcommand: see
remove-refuses-in-place-with-stdin, which passes.

Moving the index guards into validate() would fix every refusal at
once. validate() may read the file, so the block count is reachable
from there.
